WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.680
Along with displaying the genome sequence, shown&nbsp;
at the top of the genome browser image, the genome

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.120
browser also allows us to view various tracks&nbsp;
aligned with the genome sequence in the lower

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.840
portion of the browser image. A track is a visual&nbsp;
representation of a data set related to the genome

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.640
sequence above it. Tracks can show data from&nbsp;
diverse data sets, such as gene predictions mRNA

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.120
or RNA-Seq expression data, sequence similarity to&nbsp;
other organisms, and information about repeats and

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.720
variation associated with a certain region of&nbsp;
the genome. Depending on the researcher's goals,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.800
it is often helpful to display only a subset of&nbsp;
the available evidence tracks. You can control the

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.160
display mode of each track through the drop-down&nbsp;
lists at the bottom of the genome browser page.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.160
For example, the tracks that show sequence&nbsp;
similarity to other organisms and the gene models

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.280
from different gene predictors are available under&nbsp;
the Genes and Gene Prediction Tracks category.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:13.340
Each track has up to five display modes.&nbsp;
For example, if we click on the drop-down&nbsp;list for the Base Position track,

00:01:13.374 --> 00:01:23.600
the first track under the Mapping and Sequencing Tracks category, we can change the display to one of the three&nbsp;display modes available: hide, dense, or full.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.680
In contrast, if we click on the drop down list for the&nbsp;
Gap track, we will see the five display modes that

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:37.680
are available: hide, dense, squish, pack and full. To&nbsp;
better understand how the display modes affect the

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.760
Genome Browser image, we will experiment with the&nbsp;
different display settings for the D. mel Proteins

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:50.240
track. Scroll down to the genes and gene prediction&nbsp;
tracks category, change the display mode for the

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:58.652
D. mel Proteins track to "hide," and then click on the&nbsp;refresh button. "Hide" means this particular track&nbsp;will not be shown at all.

00:01:58.686 --> 00:02:09.920
Now we will change the&nbsp;display mode for the D. mel Proteins track to "dense" and then hit refresh. "Dense" means the selected&nbsp;track is displayed with all features collapsed

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:16.960
into a single line, irrespective of whether there&nbsp;
are overlapping features. In this case, you can

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.520
see that all the features in the D. mel Proteins&nbsp;
track are shown in one line. This mode is useful

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:30.080
for reducing the amount of space used by a track when you don't need to access the individual items

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.800
or when you just want to get an overall view&nbsp;
of the distribution of the features along the

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:41.200
genomic region of interest. Let's now change the&nbsp;
display mode for the D. mel Proteins track to "full."

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.440
We see that there are many more features in the&nbsp;
D. mel Protein section of the Genome Browser image.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:55.040
That is because the full display option shows&nbsp;
each annotation feature on a separate line. Because

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.120
this display mode may result in an overwhelming&nbsp;
number of items, it should only be used when you

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:08.480
are examining a small genomic region. To reduce the&nbsp;
height of the Genome Browser image, we can change

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:15.760
the display mode for the D. mel Proteins track to&nbsp;
"pack." In pack mode, the track is displayed with each

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.440
annotation feature shown separately and labeled,&nbsp;
but overlapping features are shown on separate

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.000
lines. Hence, this display mode offers a good&nbsp;
compromise between the dense and full display

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.760
modes. The final display mode, "squish," is similar&nbsp;
to pack mode, but each feature is shown at half

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.680
the height of the features in pack mode. This&nbsp;
allows the genome browser image to show more data

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:48.960
per inch of screen height. This display mode is&nbsp;
particularly useful for high density data tracks,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:59.040
such as displaying all of the RNA-Seq reads
that have been mapped to a genomic region.

